import{t as e}from"./links-ClIw6rLF.js";var t={id:`P4`,title:`Inside a Real Inverter`,level:`intermediate`,stage:`practitioner`,summary:`A guided tour of two real inverter architectures in the 3D laboratory, safe disassembly and reassembly, and component identification on the boards.`,prerequisites:[`F1`],recommended:[`F4`],status:`published`,phase:9,competency:`CU-09`,modules:[{id:`P4.M1`,title:`Architecture tour`,summary:`A guided tour of a real inverter's five functional areas in the 3D Dissection Laboratory: enclosure and terminals, power stage, magnetics, control electronics, and protection and sensing.`,lessons:[{id:`P4.M1.L1`,title:`Enclosure, terminals and earthing`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Identify an inverter's enclosure, battery/PV/AC terminals and protective earth stud in the 3D laboratory.`,`Explain why the protective earth connection is always the last removed and the first reconnected.`,`Relate each terminal to the component family it belongs to, from F1 and F4.`],introduction:`This module is a guided tour through a real inverter's internal architecture, using the two model inverters (LF-3K and SQ-1K) in the 3D Dissection Laboratory. This first lesson starts at the outside: the enclosure, its external terminals, and the [[protective-earth|protective earth]] connection that ties the whole chassis to a safe reference.`,lecture:`### The enclosure as a safety boundary, not just a box

An inverter's enclosure is not simply a container — it is the first line of defence between hazardous internal voltages (the DC bus, F1.M2) and anyone nearby. Opening the LF-3K model in the laboratory, you will find the cover held by multiple screws and bonded to the chassis by an earth strap, exactly matching the removal sequence F1 taught: isolate, discharge, THEN open the cover, never the reverse.

### External terminals, by family

Walking the terminal block, each terminal corresponds to a component family you met in F1 and F4: battery terminals (+ and −, bolted, torque-specified, F4.M5.L4), a battery [[fuse|fuse]] (F4.M4.L1) in series with the positive terminal, PV input terminals (for a solar-capable model), a DC [[breaker|circuit breaker]] (F4.M4.L2), and AC output terminals (L, N, PE). Each of these is a real, separately testable part in the Component Library (F4) — the 3D laboratory shows you WHERE it physically sits and how it connects to everything else, which a library entry alone cannot.

### Why the earth connection is special

The [[protective-earth|protective earth (PE)]] stud is wired to bond the chassis, the enclosure and every earthed terminal to one common reference. In the removal sequence, PE is deliberately the LAST connection removed when disassembling and the FIRST reconnected when reassembling — this way, every other part of the chassis remains earthed throughout the entire procedure except for the briefest possible window, minimising the time any metal part could become unexpectedly live relative to the technician.`,technical:`**Earth continuity, not just presence.** A visibly connected earth strap is not sufficient on its own — F1's earth-continuity testing (a low-resistance check between the PE stud and every bonded metal part) is what actually confirms the bond is doing its job, exactly the same test procedure used on real equipment before it is ever considered safe to work on.

**Terminal torque and the lesson this module revisits.** Every bolted terminal here (battery, DC input, AC output) is exactly the class of joint F4.M5.L4 covered: specified torque, and a voltage-drop-under-load check as the real-world way to confirm it is still sound after use, not a one-time install check.`,diagrams:[{id:`risk-hierarchy`,caption:`The enclosure and earth bond as the outermost layer of an inverter's defence, with each external terminal mapped to the component family it belongs to.`}],formulas:[{name:`Earth continuity (concept)`,expr:`R_bond should read a small fraction of an ohm, end to end, confirmed by a dedicated low-resistance test`,where:[],note:`A qualitative pass/fail check, not a calculated value — see F1's earth-continuity procedure.`}],examples:[{title:`Reasoning about removal order`,problem:`A technician removes the PE stud before disconnecting the battery terminals, to "get it out of the way first". Explain why this is the wrong order.`,steps:[`Removing PE first leaves the chassis and every other still-connected part without its earth reference for the entire remainder of the procedure, not just a brief moment`,`The battery and any other live source remain connected throughout this extended unearthed window`],answer:`This maximises, rather than minimises, the time the chassis is unearthed while still potentially live — PE should be removed LAST (after every source is isolated and discharged) and reconnected FIRST, keeping the unearthed window as short as possible.`},{title:`Matching a terminal to its component family`,problem:`While touring the LF-3K model, you find a bolted terminal block labelled "DC IN" near a MEGA-style fuse. Which F4 component families does this terminal and its neighbour belong to?`,steps:[`The terminal block itself is a DC input/battery-terminal type component (F4.M5.L4 testing)`,`The neighbouring part is a fuse, protecting that DC input circuit (F4.M4.L1)`],answer:`A DC-input terminal (tested by voltage-drop-under-load, F4.M5.L4) in series with a protective fuse (I²t and breaking-capacity rated, F4.M4.L1) — exactly the pairing F4 taught in theory, now seen as a real physical assembly.`}],applications:`Every terminal and the earth stud you identify here is the same physical part, and the same test procedure, you met in F1's safety drills and F4's component modules — this lesson is where that knowledge becomes a real, located, three-dimensional object.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating the enclosure as just a cosmetic cover.`,correction:`It is a deliberate safety boundary, and its removal sequence (isolate, discharge, then open) is not optional ceremony.`},{mistake:`Removing the earth connection early "to get it out of the way".`,correction:`PE must be the LAST connection removed and the FIRST reconnected, minimising the unearthed window.`},{mistake:`Assuming a visibly intact earth strap means the bond is electrically sound.`,correction:`A dedicated continuity test, not visual inspection, confirms the earth bond is actually doing its job.`}],demo:{title:`Tour the enclosure and terminals`,text:`Open the LF-3K model and identify each external terminal and the earth stud.`,link:e.lab3d(`Enclosure and terminals`,`lf3k`,`enclosure`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Enclosure and terminals`,`lf3k`,`enclosure`),e.lab3d(`Protective earth stud`,`lf3k`,`pe`),e.proc(`Battery terminal check`,`battery-terminals`,`terminal-drop`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The protective earth connection is removed...`,options:[`last, and reconnected first`,`first, and reconnected last`,`at any convenient point in the sequence`,`only if the model has a battery`],answer:0,explain:`Minimising the unearthed window is the whole point of this sequencing.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A visibly connected earth strap...`,options:[`still needs a continuity test to confirm it is electrically sound`,`is always electrically sound by inspection alone`,`is never tested in practice`,`replaces the need for isolation before opening the cover`],answer:0,explain:`Visual inspection alone does not confirm low-resistance continuity.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A DC input terminal block paired with a fuse in series represents...`,options:[`a terminal (F4.M5.L4) protected by a fuse (F4.M4.L1), the same pairing taught in F4`,`two unrelated, coincidental parts`,`a single component with two names`,`a fault condition`],answer:0,explain:`This is the standard, intentional terminal/fuse pairing.`}],summary:[`The enclosure is a deliberate safety boundary, removed only after isolation and discharge.`,`External terminals (battery, fuse, PV, breaker, AC output) each correspond to a real, separately testable F4 component family.`,`The protective earth connection is removed last and reconnected first, minimising the time the chassis is unearthed.`,`A continuity test, not visual inspection, is what actually confirms an earth bond is sound.`],homework:{id:`P4.M1.L1.hw`,title:`Enclosure and terminal identification`,brief:`Apply the removal-order and terminal-family reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which is removed first when disassembling: the cover, or the PE stud?`,options:[`The cover`,`The PE stud`,`Both simultaneously`,`Neither is ever removed`],answer:0,explain:`PE stays connected until every other disconnection is complete.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain, in your own words, why a technician who is confident the battery is already disconnected still should not remove the PE stud early "since the battery is safe anyway".`,model:`The earth bond's purpose is not limited to protecting against the battery specifically — it protects against ANY unexpected voltage appearing on the chassis, including from a fault, a miswired connection, or a source the technician did not anticipate (such as back-feed through the AC output terminals). Removing PE early removes this general safety margin for the rest of the procedure, regardless of how confident the technician is about the battery alone — the earth bond is protection against the unexpected, not just against the one source that has been checked.`}]}}},{id:`P4.M1.L2`,title:`The power stage`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Identify the MOSFETs, IGBT, gate drivers, bus capacitors and relay on a real power board.`,`Relate each identified part's physical placement to its electrical role from F4.M2.`,`Explain why the power-board layout places switches directly against the heat sink.`],introduction:`Having toured the outside of the inverter, this lesson moves to its functional heart: the [[pcb|power board]], where the [[mosfet|MOSFETs]], [[igbt|IGBT]], [[gate-driver|gate drivers]] and bus capacitors you studied in F4.M2 sit as real, physical, soldered parts.`,lecture:`### Reading a real power board

The LF-3K model's main PCB carries six power switches silkscreened Q1–Q6: four MOSFETs (Q1–Q4, forming the H-bridge legs) and two IGBTs (Q5–Q6, in the MPPT charger stage) — exactly the device-family split F4.M2.L4/L5 explained: MOSFETs where switching speed dominates, an IGBT-friendly stage where a different voltage/frequency trade-off applies. Alongside them sit three gate drivers (U2–U4, F4.M2.L6), one per switching leg, placed as close as practical to their respective gates to minimise stray inductance in the drive path.

### Bus capacitors, bleeders and the relay

Six capacitors (C1–C6) form the battery-bus and PV-bus smoothing banks (F4.M1.L2), each sized for its own voltage domain; two bleeder resistors (F4.M1.L1, F1.M2) sit across the higher-voltage bank specifically to discharge it safely after power-off. A relay (K1, F4.M4.L3) handles output/transfer switching — a job suited to a relay's isolation and low conduction loss rather than a semiconductor switch, exactly the reasoning F4.M4.L4 compared directly.

### Why switches are clamped to the heat sink

Every one of the six power switches is physically clamped to the main heat sink, not merely placed near it — F4.M5.L3's thermal-resistance path (junction → case → sink → ambient) only works if the THERMAL contact between device and sink is as good as the removal note warns: "all six power switches are clamped to it and must be unclamped first" before the heat sink itself can come out. This clamping is a mechanical requirement of the thermal design, not an assembly convenience.`,technical:`**Hall-effect current sensor placement.** The CT1 Hall sensor is soldered with the main battery busbar passing directly through its aperture (F4.M5.L2) — a physical arrangement that lets it sense current without breaking the circuit or adding series resistance, visibly demonstrating the isolation advantage F4.M5.L2 described in theory.

**DC input lugs soldered directly to the board.** Unlike a separate terminal block, this board's DC input lugs (J1) are soldered straight into the PCB — meaning a high-resistance joint here (F4.M5.L4) would show up as PCB heat damage around the lugs themselves, not just a loose connector, a detail worth knowing before inspecting a real board for heat damage.`,diagrams:[{id:`hbridge-states`,caption:`The power board's six switches (four MOSFETs, two IGBTs), their gate drivers, and the bus capacitors and relay around them, mapped to their F4.M2/M4 electrical roles.`}],formulas:[{name:`Removal dependency (concept)`,expr:`Switches must be unclamped before the heat sink can be removed; the heat sink must be freed before the board beneath it is accessible`,where:[],note:`A removal-ORDER relationship, not a numeric formula — see P4.M2 for the full disassembly sequence this constrains.`}],examples:[{title:`Matching silkscreen labels to device families`,problem:`The board's silkscreen shows Q1–Q4 near one driver group and Q5–Q6 near another. Based on F4.M2, what is the most likely reason these are split into two groups rather than six identical switches?`,steps:[`F4.M2.L4/L5 explained that MOSFETs and IGBTs suit different voltage/frequency trade-offs`,`A board with both an H-bridge (needing fast, repeated switching) and an MPPT charger stage (often at different voltage/frequency requirements) would plausibly use the device best suited to each stage rather than one device type for both`],answer:`Q1–Q4 (MOSFETs) likely serve the H-bridge, where switching speed matters most; Q5–Q6 (IGBTs) likely serve the MPPT charger stage, where the IGBT's conduction-loss advantage at its operating voltage is the better fit — exactly the selection reasoning F4.M2.L5 taught.`},{title:`Why the heat sink cannot be removed first`,problem:`A new technician tries to remove the heat sink before unclamping the power switches. Explain why this fails and what it reveals about the thermal design.`,steps:[`The switches are physically clamped TO the heat sink, not merely resting near it`,`Removing the heat sink while switches remain clamped would mean trying to pull the switches' thermal contact apart forcibly, risking damage to both`],answer:`The clamping must be released first — this removal dependency exists precisely BECAUSE the thermal design relies on firm, continuous mechanical contact between each switch and the heat sink (F4.M5.L3); it is not an arbitrary assembly order.`}],applications:`This lesson is the direct physical counterpart of F4.M2 (semiconductor devices) and F4.M4.L3 (relays) — the Repair Workshop's MOSFET and gate-driver scenarios are diagnosing faults on exactly this kind of real board layout.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming every power switch on a real board is the same device type.`,correction:`A real design commonly mixes MOSFETs and IGBTs, each where its own strengths suit a different stage of the circuit.`},{mistake:`Treating the heat sink as removable independently of the switches clamped to it.`,correction:`The clamping is a genuine mechanical thermal-design requirement; switches must be unclamped before the heat sink can come free.`},{mistake:`Overlooking the gate drivers' placement as incidental.`,correction:`Drivers are placed close to their respective gates deliberately, to minimise stray inductance in the drive path (F4.M2.L6).`}],demo:{title:`Tour the power board`,text:`Identify the MOSFETs, IGBT, gate drivers and bus capacitors on the LF-3K power board.`,link:e.lab3d(`Power board`,`lf3k`,`pcb-main`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Power switches`,`lf3k`,`q1`),e.lab3d(`Gate drivers`,`lf3k`,`u2`),e.lab3d(`Bus capacitors`,`lf3k`,`c1`),e.lib(`MOSFET`,`mosfet`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A real power board mixing MOSFETs and IGBTs most likely reflects...`,options:[`each device family being used where its own strengths suit a different stage of the circuit`,`a manufacturing error`,`random component selection`,`cost-cutting with no technical reason`],answer:0,explain:`Deliberate selection per F4.M2's device-comparison reasoning.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Power switches are clamped directly to the heat sink because...`,options:[`good thermal contact is a mechanical requirement of the cooling design`,`it is easier to solder them that way`,`it has no real purpose`,`it reduces their electrical rating`],answer:0,explain:`Firm mechanical contact is what makes the thermal-resistance path work.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A Hall-effect current sensor with the busbar passing through its aperture demonstrates...`,options:[`current sensing without breaking the circuit or adding series resistance`,`a wiring fault`,`a shunt-resistor sensing method`,`a voltage-sensing method`],answer:0,explain:`This is the Hall-effect isolation advantage from F4.M5.L2, seen physically.`}],summary:[`A real power board mixes MOSFETs and IGBTs, each placed where its own electrical strengths suit that stage of the circuit.`,`Gate drivers sit close to their respective switches to minimise stray inductance in the drive path.`,`Bus capacitors and bleeder resistors are sized per voltage domain, exactly as F4.M1 described.`,`Power switches are mechanically clamped to the heat sink, a genuine thermal-design requirement that constrains disassembly order.`],homework:{id:`P4.M1.L2.hw`,title:`Power board identification practice`,brief:`Apply device-family and thermal-design reasoning to the power board.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Before the heat sink can be removed from a real power board, what must happen first?`,options:[`The power switches clamped to it must be unclamped`,`The battery must be reconnected`,`The gate drivers must be re-soldered`,`Nothing; it lifts out freely at any time`],answer:0,explain:`Clamped switches must be freed before the heat sink itself is removable.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician identifies six power switches on a board and assumes, without checking further, that all six must be MOSFETs since "that's the most common power switch". Explain why this assumption could lead to an incorrect replacement if one of them fails.`,model:`A real board can deliberately mix MOSFETs and IGBTs across different stages (F4.M2.L4/L5), so assuming every switch is the same device type without checking its actual marking or datasheet risks sourcing and fitting the wrong replacement part entirely — a MOSFET fitted where an IGBT belonged (or vice versa) would likely have mismatched ratings, gate-drive characteristics, and conduction-loss behaviour, exactly the compatibility failure F4.M2.L10 warned against. Correctly identifying each specific device, not assuming uniformity, is essential before any replacement.`}]}}},{id:`P4.M1.L3`,title:`Magnetics`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Identify the transformer, output inductor and output filter capacitor on a real power board.`,`Relate their physical size and mounting to the design considerations from F4.M3.`,`Explain why the transformer is one of the last parts removed in disassembly.`],introduction:`This lesson tours the [[transformer|transformer]], output inductor and output filter capacitor — the magnetic components of F4.M3, now seen as real, substantial physical parts rather than symbols on a schematic.`,lecture:`### The transformer: seen and felt

The LF-3K model's transformer (T1) is an EI-core, line-frequency design rated 3 kVA, 30/230 V — and its removal note states its mass directly: roughly 25 kg, requiring two hands and four clamp-foot bolts to remove. This is F4.M3.L2's LF-versus-HF size comparison made completely concrete: a line-frequency transformer really is this large and heavy for its power rating, which is exactly why an HF-link design (achieving the same power transformation with a small ferrite core) is so much lighter in practice, not just in theory.

### The output filter: inductor and capacitor together

Immediately after the transformer's secondary comes the output filter: a toroidal inductor (L1, rated 1.2 mH, 15 A) and a film capacitor (C7, rated 10 µF, 275 V AC) — the exact LC pairing F4.M3.L3 described, sized together to set a corner frequency that passes the 50 Hz fundamental while attenuating switching-frequency content. Their physical ordering on the board — transformer, then inductor, then capacitor, following the actual current path — mirrors the signal-flow order you would trace on a schematic.

### Why the transformer comes out near-last

The transformer's own removal note explains the dependency directly: its leads connect to the output filter, which must be removed first, and only then can the transformer itself — the heaviest part — be lifted out. This is a genuine structural/electrical dependency (the filter parts are wired to the transformer and must be disconnected before it can move), not an arbitrary sequencing choice, and it is exactly the kind of removal-order reasoning P4.M2 will formalise into a complete disassembly sequence.`,technical:`**Core material, visibly.** The EI-core geometry (laminated steel, F4.M3.L2) is visually distinct from the output inductor's toroidal ferrite/powder core — two different core technologies, chosen for two different jobs (line-frequency isolation versus high-frequency-content filtering), sitting a few centimetres apart on the same board.

**Why the output capacitor is a film type, not electrolytic.** The output filter capacitor is specifically a film capacitor (F4.M1.L2), not an electrolytic — film types tolerate the AC ripple current and reversing voltage at the switching/output frequency far better than a polarised electrolytic would, which is why this specific capacitor family is used at exactly this point in the circuit.`,diagrams:[{id:`lc-filter`,caption:`The transformer, output inductor and output filter capacitor in physical sequence on the power board, mirroring the signal-flow order of F4.M3's LC filter stage.`}],formulas:[{name:`Removal dependency (concept)`,expr:`Output filter parts (inductor, capacitor) must be disconnected before the transformer, which they are wired to, can be lifted free`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Relating mass to transformer technology`,problem:`The LF-3K's transformer is rated 3 kVA and weighs roughly 25 kg. Using F4.M3.L2's reasoning, estimate (qualitatively, not numerically) how an equivalent-power HF-link design's magnetics would compare in mass.`,steps:[`F4.M3.L2 explained that HF transformer size falls sharply as switching frequency rises, for the same power, because far less flux swing per cycle is needed`,`An HF-link transformer of the same power rating would therefore need a dramatically smaller, lighter core than this 25 kg line-frequency unit`],answer:`An equivalent-power HF-link design's transformer would be expected to weigh a small fraction of 25 kg — this real part's mass is a direct, physical demonstration of the LF-versus-HF trade-off F4.M3.L2 covered in theory.`},{title:`Why film, not electrolytic, at the output filter`,problem:`Explain why the output filter capacitor (C7) is a film type rather than the electrolytic type used for the DC bus capacitors elsewhere on the board.`,steps:[`The output filter capacitor sees AC ripple current and a reversing (AC-like) voltage at the output/switching frequency`,`An electrolytic capacitor is polarised and generally tolerates AC/reversing voltage and high ripple current poorly compared with a film type (F4.M1.L2)`],answer:`A film capacitor is selected specifically because it tolerates the reversing voltage and AC ripple current at this point in the circuit far better than an electrolytic would — matching the capacitor type to its actual operating conditions, not just its capacitance value.`}],applications:`The transformer-versus-inductor comparison guide and the magnetics-related Repair Workshop scenarios both draw directly on exactly this physical layout and reasoning.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming the transformer can be removed as soon as its mounting bolts are visible.`,correction:`Its leads connect to the output filter, which must be disconnected first — a genuine wiring dependency, not just a bolt-removal order.`},{mistake:`Treating all capacitors on a board as interchangeable once their capacitance value matches.`,correction:`Capacitor TYPE (film versus electrolytic) must match the actual operating conditions (AC ripple, reversing voltage) at that specific point in the circuit.`},{mistake:`Assuming a transformer's size is simply a manufacturing choice unrelated to its switching frequency.`,correction:`Size scales directly with required flux swing per cycle, which is set by operating frequency — this is physics, not an arbitrary design preference.`}],demo:{title:`Tour the magnetics`,text:`See the transformer, output inductor and filter capacitor on the LF-3K power board.`,link:e.lab3d(`Transformer and output filter`,`lf3k`,`t1`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Transformer`,`lf3k`,`t1`),e.lab3d(`Output inductor`,`lf3k`,`l1`),e.cmp(`Transformer vs inductor`,`transformer-vs-inductor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The LF-3K transformer's substantial mass (~25 kg) for 3 kVA directly illustrates...`,options:[`why line-frequency transformers are much larger than HF-link designs of equal power`,`a manufacturing defect`,`an oversized, poorly designed part`,`nothing relevant to F4.M3`],answer:0,explain:`Mass is a direct physical consequence of operating at line frequency.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The output filter capacitor is a film type rather than electrolytic because...`,options:[`it must tolerate AC ripple current and reversing voltage at this point in the circuit`,`film capacitors are always cheaper`,`electrolytics cannot be used anywhere in an inverter`,`capacitance value is the only consideration`],answer:0,explain:`Capacitor type must match actual operating conditions.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The transformer is removed near-last in the disassembly sequence because...`,options:[`its leads connect to the output filter, which must be disconnected first`,`it is the lightest part on the board`,`it has no electrical connections`,`removal order has no real constraint here`],answer:0,explain:`A genuine wiring dependency, not an arbitrary choice.`}],summary:[`The transformer's substantial physical size and mass directly demonstrate the LF-versus-HF trade-off F4.M3.L2 covered in theory.`,`The output inductor and filter capacitor sit immediately after the transformer, mirroring the LC filter's actual signal-flow order.`,`The output filter capacitor is a film type specifically because it tolerates AC ripple and reversing voltage better than an electrolytic.`,`The transformer's removal depends on first disconnecting the output filter parts wired to it — a genuine structural dependency.`],homework:{id:`P4.M1.L3.hw`,title:`Magnetics identification practice`,brief:`Apply LF/HF and capacitor-type reasoning to the real board.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An output filter capacitor seeing AC ripple and reversing voltage should be a...`,options:[`film capacitor`,`electrolytic capacitor`,`ceramic capacitor only`,`any capacitor type, it makes no difference`],answer:0,explain:`Film capacitors tolerate these conditions far better than electrolytics.`}],written:[{prompt:`A student argues that since the transformer and the output inductor are both "magnetic components", they could be removed in either order without consequence. Explain why the actual wiring dependency makes this incorrect.`,model:`Being in the same general category (magnetic components, F4.M3) does not mean two parts are interchangeable in a removal sequence — what matters is their actual WIRING relationship, not their component family. Here, the output inductor and filter capacitor are wired directly to the transformer's secondary leads, so the transformer cannot be freed and lifted out until those connections are disconnected; attempting to remove the transformer first would mean pulling on wires still attached to the inductor and capacitor, risking damage to the connections or the parts themselves. The correct removal order follows the actual electrical dependency, not a generic grouping by component category.`}]}}},{id:`P4.M1.L4`,title:`Control electronics`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Identify the microcontroller, PWM controller, voltage-sense module and display board on a real control board.`,`Relate each part's physical connection (ribbon cable, header pins, solder) to how it is removed and tested.`,`Explain why the control board sits above the power board's cable path in the removal sequence.`],introduction:`This lesson tours the [[microcontroller|microcontroller]], [[pwm-controller|PWM controller]] and voltage-sensing module — the control electronics of F4.M5.L1-L2 — together with the display board, as real parts on their own dedicated control board.`,lecture:`### Two control philosophies, side by side

The LF-3K model's control board carries BOTH control philosophies F4.M5.L1 compared: a 32-bit microcontroller (U6, fine-pitch LQFP64 package, running firmware) handling the main inversion control, and a dedicated PWM controller IC (U1, a through-hole DIP-16 SG3525-family part) handling the simpler MPPT charger stage — exactly the "use the right tool for each job" reasoning F4.M5.L1 taught, now visible as two different IC packages doing two different control jobs on the same board.

### Connection types tell you how a part is removed and tested

Different parts connect differently, and this directly determines how they come off: the microcontroller and PWM controller are soldered (the MCU fine-pitch, the PWM controller in a socket), the voltage-sense module is a plug-in unit on header pins (unplug its high-voltage sense leads first, since it floats at a different reference, F4.M5.L2's isolation discussion), and the display board connects via a ribbon cable with a latch (release the latch, don't pull the ribbon). Reading a part's connection type before touching it is the real-world equivalent of checking a datasheet before testing a component out of circuit (F4.M2.L8).

### Why the control board comes off before the power board

The removal sequence takes the control board's own components off first (voltage-sense module unplugged, MCU and PWM controller noted for inspection), then the control board itself, specifically because it sits ABOVE the power board's cable path — leaving it in place would block access to what is underneath. This is a spatial, not electrical, constraint, but it is just as real a dependency as the transformer/output-filter wiring dependency from P4.M1.L3.`,technical:`**Isolated programmer for firmware work.** If the microcontroller's firmware needs reading or reprogramming on real equipment, an isolated programmer is required, since the control ground may sit at battery potential rather than earth — a safety detail directly tied to F4.M5.L1's point that the control path is not automatically isolated from the power stage.

**A blank display is not necessarily a display fault.** The display board's own test procedure (F4.M5) checks its supply voltage AND its serial communication separately — a blank display with healthy supply voltage points to a communication fault (cable or MCU) rather than the display board itself, exactly the "check the sensor/signal before assuming the obvious part" discipline F4.M5.L2 taught for current sensors.`,diagrams:[{id:`feedback-loop`,caption:`The microcontroller and PWM controller on the control board implementing the closed feedback loop of F4.M5.L1, alongside the voltage-sense module and display board.`}],formulas:[{name:`Removal dependency (concept)`,expr:`Control-board components are removed, then the control board itself, before the power board beneath it becomes accessible`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Why two different control ICs appear on one board`,problem:`Explain, using F4.M5.L1, why this board uses a microcontroller for the main inversion control but a simpler dedicated PWM IC for the charger stage, rather than one microcontroller doing both jobs.`,steps:[`F4.M5.L1 explained that a dedicated PWM IC offers simple, predictable, fixed-function control with no firmware risk`,`The charger stage is plausibly a simpler, more fixed function than the main inversion control, which benefits from the microcontroller's flexibility`],answer:`Using a dedicated PWM IC for the simpler, fixed-function charger stage avoids adding unnecessary firmware complexity and risk there, while reserving the microcontroller's flexibility for the main control function that actually needs it — matching each control philosophy to the job it suits, exactly as F4.M5.L1 argued in the abstract.`},{title:`Diagnosing a blank display using connection-type knowledge`,problem:`A display is blank. Its ribbon cable is confirmed properly latched. What should be checked next, and why, rather than assuming the display board itself has failed?`,steps:[`The display's own test procedure checks supply voltage and serial communication as two SEPARATE steps`,`A properly latched cable with power present but no communication points toward a communication-path or MCU fault, not necessarily the display hardware`],answer:`Check the supply voltage at the display board connector first, then look for serial activity on the data line with a scope — only if BOTH are healthy and the display is still blank does the display board itself become the more likely suspect, rather than being assumed first.`}],applications:`This lesson is the physical counterpart of F4.M5.L1-L2, and the Repair Workshop's gate-driver-signal-lost scenario traces a fault through exactly this kind of control-board layout.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a board with both a microcontroller and a dedicated PWM IC has one redundant/unnecessary control chip.`,correction:`Each handles a different function, matched to the control philosophy (flexible firmware vs simple fixed-function) that function actually needs.`},{mistake:`Pulling a ribbon cable free without releasing its latch first.`,correction:`A half-seated or forcibly removed ribbon connection is a known, preventable cause of exactly the blank-display symptom this lesson discusses.`},{mistake:`Diagnosing a blank display as a display-board fault before checking its supply and communication separately.`,correction:`The display's own healthy-vs-faulty readings are split across two independent checks — jumping to "replace the display" skips evidence that would narrow the actual cause.`}],demo:{title:`Tour the control board`,text:`Identify the microcontroller, PWM controller, voltage-sense module and display board.`,link:e.lab3d(`Control board`,`lf3k`,`u6`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Microcontroller`,`lf3k`,`u6`),e.lab3d(`PWM controller`,`lf3k`,`u1`),e.lab3d(`Display board`,`lf3k`,`disp`),e.proc(`Display board check`,`display-board`,`display-check`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`This board uses both a microcontroller and a dedicated PWM IC because...`,options:[`each control philosophy is matched to the specific job it suits best`,`one of them is redundant and unused`,`microcontrollers cannot generate PWM at all`,`dedicated PWM ICs cannot be used alongside microcontrollers`],answer:0,explain:`Deliberate matching of control technology to function, per F4.M5.L1.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A blank display with confirmed-healthy supply voltage and a properly latched cable most likely points to...`,options:[`a serial communication fault`,`definitely a failed display board`,`a power-stage MOSFET fault`,`normal operation`],answer:0,explain:`Supply and communication are separately tested; a healthy supply shifts suspicion toward communication.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The control board is removed before the power board beneath it mainly because...`,options:[`it physically sits above the power board's cable path, blocking access`,`it has no electrical connections`,`the power board is never actually removed`,`removal order has no constraint here`],answer:0,explain:`A spatial access dependency, not an electrical one.`}],summary:[`A real control board can carry both a flexible microcontroller and a simple dedicated PWM IC, each matched to the function it suits.`,`Each part's connection type (soldered, plug-in header, ribbon cable) determines how it is safely removed and tested.`,`A blank display is diagnosed by checking supply and communication separately, not assumed to be a display-board fault.`,`The control board's removal before the power board reflects a spatial access dependency, distinct from the electrical dependencies seen in P4.M1.L3.`],homework:{id:`P4.M1.L4.hw`,title:`Control electronics identification practice`,brief:`Apply connection-type and fault-separation reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A voltage-sense module's high-voltage sense leads should be unplugged...`,options:[`before the module itself is removed from its header pins`,`only after the module is already removed`,`never, it is permanently soldered`,`at the very end of the whole disassembly`],answer:0,explain:`The module floats at a different reference; its sense leads come off first.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician sees a microcontroller and a separate PWM controller IC on the same board and assumes the PWM controller is a legacy leftover from an older design that the microcontroller has since made redundant. Explain why this assumption is not safe to act on without further investigation.`,model:`Two control ICs coexisting on a board is not, by itself, evidence that one is obsolete — F4.M5.L1 explained that a dedicated PWM IC is often deliberately retained for a specific, simpler, fixed-function role (such as a charger stage) precisely because it avoids the firmware complexity and risk a microcontroller would add there, even in a design whose main control is otherwise microcontroller-based. Removing or disabling the PWM controller on the assumption that it is redundant, without first confirming what function it actually serves in this specific board's design, risks disabling a function the microcontroller was never intended to handle.`}]}}},{id:`P4.M1.L5`,title:`Protection and sensing`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Identify the fuse, breaker, current sensor and voltage-sense module as a coordinated protection/sensing layer.`,`Relate their physical placement to the feedback and protection reasoning from F4.M4-M5.`,`Explain why protection and sensing are reviewed together, not as separate, unrelated systems.`],introduction:`This closing lesson of the architecture tour brings together the [[fuse|fuse]], [[breaker|breaker]], [[current-sensor|current sensor]] and voltage-sense module as one coordinated system — protection (F4.M4) and sensing/feedback (F4.M5.L1-L2) are physically distributed across the board, but functionally they work together.`,lecture:`### Protection devices, located

The battery fuse sits in series with the positive battery terminal, immediately downstream of the terminal block — exactly where F4.M4.L1 would place it, as close as practical to the source it protects. The DC breaker sits on the PV input path, where its resettable nature (F4.M4.L2) suits a circuit that may trip occasionally from normal PV-related transients without needing a part replaced each time.

### Sensing, located

The Hall-effect current sensor (CT1) is positioned with the main battery busbar passing through its aperture — current sensing without adding series resistance or breaking the circuit, physically demonstrating the isolation advantage F4.M5.L2 described. The voltage-sense module sits on the control board as a plug-in unit specifically because it must handle a high-voltage reference safely isolated from the low-voltage control logic around it.

### Why protection and sensing are reviewed together

Both systems watch the SAME electrical quantities (voltage and current) for different purposes: protection devices (fuses, breakers) act independently and mechanically when a quantity exceeds a hard physical limit; sensors feed the SAME kind of quantity to the control loop (F4.M5.L1) for continuous, software-mediated correction. A fault in either can produce overlapping symptoms — F4.M5.L2 showed how a drifted sensor can mimic an overcurrent condition that looks like it should trip the breaker but is, in fact, a sensing fault the breaker never sees at all, since the breaker and the sensor are independent systems measuring the same current for different reasons.`,technical:`**Independence as a safety principle.** Protection devices (fuses, breakers) are deliberately independent of the control/sensing system — they trip on the ACTUAL current or voltage, regardless of whether the control loop's sensors are reporting correctly. This independence is what allows a fuse to still protect the circuit even during a sensing-path fault that has fooled the control loop into misbehaving (F4.M5.L2).

**Where this layout would show up in the Repair Workshop.** A scenario presenting "overcurrent trips with no obvious cause" on this kind of board is exactly where this lesson's protection/sensing distinction matters: confirming whether the fuse/breaker tripped from a genuine overcurrent, or whether a sensor fault misled the control loop into a behaviour that merely resembles one, is the key diagnostic fork.`,diagrams:[{id:`protection-chain`,caption:`Protection devices (fuse, breaker) and sensing devices (current sensor, voltage-sense module) watching the same electrical quantities independently, for different purposes.`}],formulas:[{name:`Independence principle (concept)`,expr:`Protection devices trip on actual current/voltage, independent of what the sensing/control system reports`,where:[],note:`A qualitative design principle, not a numeric formula — the basis of why protection still works during a sensing fault.`}],examples:[{title:`Distinguishing a protection trip from a sensing fault`,problem:`A unit's breaker has tripped. Load current, measured independently with a clamp meter at the time of the trip, was well within normal range. Explain what this tells you.`,steps:[`A breaker trips on the ACTUAL current it sees, independent of the control system's own sensor readings`,`If independently measured current was genuinely normal, the breaker tripping suggests either a brief transient the clamp meter missed, or a fault in the breaker itself — not a sensing fault, since the breaker does not rely on the voltage-sense/current-sense modules at all`],answer:`Since the breaker is independent of the sensing system, this points toward a genuine (if possibly brief) overcurrent event or breaker fault, not a sensor-reporting fault — a useful diagnostic fork this lesson's independence principle makes possible.`},{title:`Why a sensing fault would not stop the fuse protecting the circuit`,problem:`A current sensor fails and begins reporting a falsely LOW current to the control loop, causing it to drive excessive duty cycle. Explain why the circuit's fuse can still protect it despite this sensing fault.`,steps:[`The fuse responds to the ACTUAL current flowing through it, from I²R heating of its own element`,`It has no electrical connection to, or dependency on, the current-sensor's reading at all`],answer:`The fuse will still open if the actual current (now elevated because the control loop was misled into driving harder) exceeds its rating — its protection function is completely independent of the sensor fault that caused the problem in the first place, which is exactly the safety value of this independence.`}],applications:`This lesson's protection/sensing distinction is directly tested by the Repair Workshop scenarios that combine an apparent overcurrent symptom with a healthy power stage — exactly the diagnostic fork this lesson sets up.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a tripped breaker always means the sensing/control system detected a problem correctly.`,correction:`The breaker trips independently of the sensing system — it responds to the actual current, not to what any sensor reports.`},{mistake:`Assuming a sensing fault would leave the circuit unprotected.`,correction:`Protection devices (fuses, breakers) remain independently functional even during a sensing-path fault, by design.`},{mistake:`Treating "protection" and "sensing" as unrelated board features with nothing in common.`,correction:`Both watch the same underlying electrical quantities, for different purposes — understanding both together, not in isolation, is what resolves overlapping fault symptoms.`}],demo:{title:`Tour the protection and sensing layer`,text:`Locate the fuse, breaker, current sensor and voltage-sense module together.`,link:e.lab3d(`Protection and sensing`,`lf3k`,`hall`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Current sensor`,`lf3k`,`hall`),e.lab3d(`Battery fuse`,`lf3k`,`fuse`),e.lab3d(`PV breaker`,`lf3k`,`breaker`),e.repair(`Gate-driver signal lost`,`rsc-10`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse protects a circuit...`,options:[`independently of what the sensing/control system reports`,`only if the current sensor confirms the overcurrent first`,`only when the microcontroller commands it to trip`,`never, protection always requires sensing input`],answer:0,explain:`Fuses and breakers act on actual current, with no dependency on the sensing system.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A current sensor reporting falsely low current while a fuse still protects the circuit demonstrates...`,options:[`the independence of protection devices from the sensing/control system`,`a design flaw`,`that fuses require sensor confirmation`,`that the sensor and fuse are the same device`],answer:0,explain:`This independence is a deliberate, valuable safety property.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Protection and sensing are reviewed together in this lesson because...`,options:[`both watch the same underlying electrical quantities, for different purposes`,`they are actually the same device`,`protection devices do not exist on real boards`,`sensing has no relationship to the quantities protection devices watch`],answer:0,explain:`Shared quantities, different purposes — hence overlapping fault symptoms.`}],summary:[`Protection devices (fuses, breakers) are placed close to what they protect and act independently of the sensing/control system.`,`Sensing devices (current sensor, voltage-sense module) feed the control loop for continuous correction, a different purpose from protection.`,`Both systems watch the same underlying electrical quantities, which is why a sensing fault can produce symptoms resembling a protection-related event.`,`Protection devices remain functional during a sensing-path fault, by design — a genuine safety property, not a coincidence.`],homework:{id:`P4.M1.L5.hw`,title:`Protection and sensing reasoning practice`,brief:`Apply the independence principle to a short scenario.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A breaker trips while an independently-measured current confirms a genuine overcurrent occurred. This is most consistent with...`,options:[`the protection system correctly doing its job`,`a sensing fault only`,`a display fault`,`no real event having occurred`],answer:0,explain:`Independent confirmation of genuine overcurrent is consistent with correct protection behaviour.`}],written:[{prompt:`Summarise, in your own words, why this lesson treats protection devices and sensing devices as two related but functionally independent systems, rather than as one combined protective function.`,model:`Protection devices (fuses, breakers) and sensing devices (current and voltage sensors) both monitor the same underlying electrical quantities, but they serve different purposes and operate through entirely different mechanisms: protection devices respond mechanically or thermally to the actual current or voltage they experience, with no dependency on any sensor reading, while sensing devices feed measurements into a software-mediated control loop for continuous correction. Treating them as functionally independent — rather than as one combined system — is what explains both why protection still works during a sensing fault, and why a sensing fault can produce symptoms that superficially resemble a protection event without actually being one; conflating the two would make exactly this kind of fault much harder to diagnose correctly.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The protective earth connection is removed...`,options:[`last, and reconnected first`,`first, and reconnected last`,`at any convenient point`,`only on models without a battery`],answer:0,explain:`Minimising the unearthed window.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A real power board mixing MOSFETs and IGBTs most likely reflects...`,options:[`each device family used where its strengths suit a different stage`,`a manufacturing error`,`random selection`,`cost-cutting with no technical basis`],answer:0,explain:`Deliberate device selection per F4.M2.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The LF-3K transformer's substantial mass for its power rating directly illustrates...`,options:[`why line-frequency transformers are larger than HF-link designs of equal power`,`a design flaw`,`an oversized part`,`nothing relevant to F4.M3`],answer:0,explain:`A direct physical consequence of line-frequency operation.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`This board uses both a microcontroller and a dedicated PWM IC because...`,options:[`each control philosophy is matched to the job it suits best`,`one is a redundant leftover`,`microcontrollers cannot generate PWM`,`PWM ICs cannot coexist with microcontrollers`],answer:0,explain:`Deliberate matching per F4.M5.L1.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse protects a circuit...`,options:[`independently of what the sensing/control system reports`,`only with sensor confirmation`,`only when commanded by firmware`,`never without sensing input`],answer:0,explain:`Protection devices act on actual current, independent of sensing.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`P4.M2`,title:`Safe disassembly and reassembly`,summary:`The complete, guided isolation, discharge, disassembly and reassembly sequence for a real inverter, performed in the 3D laboratory and verified with a functional test.`,lessons:[{id:`P4.M2.L1`,title:`Isolation and discharge in practice`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Perform the full isolation sequence (battery, PV, AC output) on a real model before opening it.`,`Confirm bus-capacitor discharge using the discharge simulator and a meter, not by assumption.`,`Explain why discharge must be CONFIRMED, not merely expected after a wait time.`],introduction:`This lesson puts F1's isolation and discharge drills into practice on a real inverter model: disconnecting every source in the correct order, then confirming — not assuming — that the bus capacitors are safe to work on.`,lecture:`### Isolating every source, in order

An inverter can have more than one source feeding its internal bus: the battery, a PV array, and even the AC output itself (which can be back-fed from a connected load or generator in some configurations). F1's isolation drill applies here directly: disconnect the battery, then the PV input, then verify the AC output is also isolated — tag the isolation point as you go, exactly as a real safe-isolation procedure requires.

### Discharge: confirm, don't assume

Bus capacitors (F4.M1.L2) store energy even after every source is disconnected, and a "wait a few minutes" rule of thumb is not a substitute for actually checking. This lesson's discharge simulator reproduces exactly what F1 taught: run the discharge check, then confirm with a (simulated) meter reading across the bus that voltage has actually fallen to a safe level, before touching anything inside.

### Why confirmation matters more here than almost anywhere else

A capacitor bank that SHOULD have discharged but has not (due to a failed bleeder resistor, F4.M1.L1, or a resistor that was never actually connected) is invisible to a visual inspection — it looks exactly like a safely discharged bank. Only a measurement reveals the difference, which is precisely why "confirm, don't assume" is the single most important habit this lesson builds.

### Carrying this habit beyond the laboratory

This is not a laboratory-only exercise. Every real repair job on inverter equipment begins with exactly this sequence, performed on a physical unit rather than a 3D model: isolate every source in order, then measure — not estimate — the bus voltage before any cover comes off or any probe goes near an internal connection. A technician who treats the simulator's discharge check as a box-ticking exercise, rather than internalising the underlying discipline, has learned the wrong lesson entirely; the simulated meter reading here should feel exactly as consequential as a real one, because the habit it is building is meant to transfer directly, unchanged, to real equipment later in a technician's career.`,technical:`**Why the discharge time can vary.** A bus capacitor's discharge time constant depends on its capacitance AND the bleeder resistor's value — a failed or disconnected bleeder resistor (F4.M1.L1's testing lesson) can leave a bank charged far longer than the "normal" wait time a technician might expect, which is exactly why a measurement, not a stopwatch, is the real safety check.

**PV-bus versus battery-bus discharge.** The LF-3K model's PV-bus capacitors (C5–C6, rated for a higher voltage than the battery-bus bank C1–C4) have their own dedicated bleeder resistors (F4.M1.L2) — each voltage domain is discharged and confirmed independently, not assumed safe just because the OTHER bank has already been confirmed discharged.`,diagrams:[{id:`isolation-flow`,caption:`The isolation sequence (battery, PV, AC output) followed by discharge confirmation at the bus capacitors, before any cover is opened.`}],formulas:[{name:`RC discharge (concept)`,expr:`V(t) = V₀ · e^(−t / (R·C))`,where:[[`V(t)`,`capacitor voltage after time t`,`V`],[`R`,`bleeder resistor value`,`Ω`],[`C`,`capacitor value`,`F`]],note:`A larger R (e.g. a failed/open bleeder behaving as a much higher resistance) makes discharge far slower than expected — hence the need to measure, not guess.`}],examples:[{title:`Why a "wait 5 minutes" rule can fail`,problem:`A technician always waits exactly 5 minutes after power-off before opening a cover, with no meter check. Using the RC discharge concept, explain a scenario where this is unsafe.`,steps:[`Discharge time depends on R (bleeder resistor) × C (capacitance)`,`If the bleeder resistor has failed open (F4.M1.L1), the effective R becomes very large, making the RC time constant far longer than 5 minutes`],answer:`With a failed bleeder resistor, the bus capacitor could remain dangerously charged well beyond 5 minutes, even though the fixed-wait rule assumes a healthy resistor every time — this is exactly why a meter confirmation, not a timer, is the real safety check.`},{title:`Confirming two voltage domains independently`,problem:`A model has both a battery-bus bank (C1–C4) and a separate PV-bus bank (C5–C6) at a higher voltage. The battery-bus bank is confirmed discharged. Is it now safe to work near the PV-bus bank too?`,steps:[`Each bank has its OWN bleeder resistor and discharge path, independent of the other`,`Confirming one bank says nothing about the other bank's actual state`],answer:`No — the PV-bus bank must be confirmed discharged independently, with its own meter reading, before it is considered safe; one bank's confirmed-safe state does not transfer to the other.`}],applications:`This exact confirm-don't-assume discipline is what the Repair Workshop's safe-isolation scenarios test directly, and it is the first, non-negotiable step before any of P4.M2's later disassembly steps.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating a fixed wait time as equivalent to a discharge confirmation.`,correction:`A failed bleeder resistor can make real discharge time far longer than any fixed wait — only a measurement confirms it.`},{mistake:`Assuming one bus capacitor bank being discharged means all banks are safe.`,correction:`Each voltage domain (e.g. battery-bus and PV-bus) has its own discharge path and must be confirmed independently.`},{mistake:`Skipping AC-output isolation because "the inverter is off anyway".`,correction:`Back-feed through the output is a real isolation hazard this lesson's sequence explicitly accounts for, independent of the inverter's own power state.`}],demo:{title:`Run the discharge check`,text:`Isolate every source, then confirm bus-capacitor discharge with the simulator and meter.`,link:e.lab3d(`Isolation and discharge`,`lf3k`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Isolation and discharge`,`lf3k`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fixed "wait 5 minutes" rule, with no meter check, is unsafe because...`,options:[`a failed bleeder resistor can make real discharge time much longer than expected`,`capacitors never actually need to discharge`,`5 minutes is always too long`,`bleeder resistors cannot fail`],answer:0,explain:`Discharge time depends on R×C; a failed resistor changes R unpredictably.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Confirming the battery-bus bank is discharged tells you...`,options:[`nothing definite about a separate PV-bus bank's state`,`that every bank in the unit is now safe`,`that the AC output is also isolated`,`that the bleeder resistors can be removed`],answer:0,explain:`Each voltage domain must be confirmed independently.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`AC output isolation is included in this sequence because...`,options:[`back-feed through the output is a real isolation hazard`,`AC terminals are never actually live`,`it is purely a formality with no real risk`,`the battery is the only possible source`],answer:0,explain:`A back-fed output is a genuine source the sequence must account for.`}],summary:[`Every source — battery, PV, AC output — is isolated in sequence before any cover is opened.`,`Discharge must be CONFIRMED with a measurement, never assumed from a fixed wait time.`,`A failed bleeder resistor can make real discharge time far longer than expected, invisibly.`,`Each voltage domain (battery-bus, PV-bus) is confirmed discharged independently.`],homework:{id:`P4.M2.L1.hw`,title:`Isolation and discharge practice`,brief:`Apply the confirm-don't-assume discipline.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The correct way to confirm a bus capacitor is discharged is to...`,options:[`measure its voltage directly`,`wait a fixed amount of time`,`look at it visually`,`assume it based on the model number`],answer:0,explain:`Only a direct measurement confirms actual voltage.`}],written:[{prompt:`A colleague says "I’ve done this a hundred times, the caps are always discharged by the time I open the cover, so I don’t bother checking anymore." Explain the flaw in this reasoning.`,model:`Past experience with HEALTHY bleeder resistors does not guarantee the CURRENT unit's bleeder resistor is still functioning — a bleeder resistor, like any other component, can fail open over time or during a previous repair, and when it does, the capacitor bank will not discharge in the expected time even though it always has before. Relying on "it's always been fine" substitutes a statistical pattern from past units for an actual measurement on THIS unit, and the one time the pattern breaks is exactly when skipping the measurement becomes dangerous. The checking habit exists precisely because failures are rare but not impossible, and their consequence (a damaging short-circuit spark or shock) is severe enough that the small, repeated cost of checking is always worth it.`}]}}},{id:`P4.M2.L2`,title:`Disassembly sequence and why`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Follow the complete guided disassembly sequence for a real inverter model.`,`Explain the reasoning behind each major step's position in the sequence.`,`Distinguish a genuine structural/electrical removal dependency from an arbitrary ordering choice.`],introduction:`With isolation and discharge confirmed, this lesson works through the complete disassembly sequence: cover, fan, switches and heat sink, power board, magnetics, and terminals — and WHY each step sits where it does.`,lecture:`### The sequence, and the reasoning behind each step

1. **Cover** — removed first, once isolation and discharge are confirmed (P4.M2.L1); access to everything else depends on it.
2. **Fan** — removed early because it physically blocks access to the heat sink; its own connection (plug plus screws) is simple and has no dependency on anything else.
3. **Switches, then heat sink** — the power switches (MOSFETs/IGBTs, P4.M1.L2) must be UNCLAMPED from the heat sink before the heat sink itself can be freed; this is a genuine mechanical dependency, not an arbitrary order.
4. **Power board** — capacitors, the oscillator/control ICs and other soldered parts are desoldered or disconnected, THEN the board itself is lifted; the board cannot come free while parts remain attached to it.
5. **Magnetics (transformer, inductor)** — the output filter (inductor, capacitor) is disconnected before the transformer, since the transformer's own leads connect to the filter (P4.M1.L3); the heaviest part, it is handled with both hands or lifting equipment.
6. **Terminals and earth stud** — removed last, in the SAME order as the original isolation sequence in reverse: AC/fuse/battery terminals, then the PE stud LAST (P4.M1.L1's reasoning restated at the physical-disassembly level).

### Dependency versus convention

Some of this order is a genuine DEPENDENCY (you cannot physically remove the heat sink while switches remain clamped to it; the transformer cannot come free while its leads are still connected to the filter). Other parts of the order are a sensible CONVENTION (removing the fan before the heat sink is easier, but not strictly required by any dependency) — recognising which is which helps a technician adapt safely when a real unit's layout differs slightly from the model.`,technical:`**Why the control board comes off before the power board in some designs.** As P4.M1.L4 noted, a control board mounted ABOVE the power board's cable path must be cleared first simply for ACCESS — a spatial dependency, distinct from the mechanical (switch-to-heat-sink) and electrical (transformer-to-filter) dependencies in this sequence.

**Documenting as you go.** Photographing or noting wire positions and orientations before disconnecting them (a habit from any real electronics repair) is what makes an otherwise-correct disassembly sequence actually reversible during reassembly — the sequence alone does not capture every wire's exact routing.`,diagrams:[{id:`risk-hierarchy`,caption:`The disassembly sequence's major steps, each annotated with whether its position reflects a genuine dependency or a sensible convention.`}],formulas:[{name:`Dependency rule (concept)`,expr:`A part cannot be removed while another part it is mechanically clamped to, or electrically wired to, remains attached`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Classifying a step as dependency or convention`,problem:`In the sequence above, classify "removing the fan before the heat sink" and "unclamping switches before removing the heat sink" as dependency or convention, with reasoning.`,steps:[`The fan merely sits in front of the heat sink and blocks convenient access, but could, in principle, be left and worked around`,`The switches are physically CLAMPED to the heat sink; the heat sink genuinely cannot be removed while they remain clamped`],answer:`Fan-before-heat-sink is a CONVENTION (practical convenience, not a hard requirement); unclamping switches before the heat sink is a genuine DEPENDENCY (mechanically impossible otherwise) — recognising this distinction helps a technician adapt the sequence safely to a slightly different real unit.`},{title:`Reasoning about reversed terminal removal order`,problem:`Explain why the terminal/earth removal step in disassembly mirrors the ORIGINAL isolation sequence, but in a specific relationship to the earth stud specifically.`,steps:[`Isolation disconnects sources; the earth stud is NOT a source and was never part of that disconnection sequence`,`In disassembly, the earth stud is removed LAST among the terminals (P4.M1.L1), keeping the chassis earthed as long as possible`],answer:`The source terminals (battery, PV, AC) are removed following the same reasoning as isolation, while the PE stud is specifically removed LAST of all of them — preserving the earth bond for as much of the procedure as physically possible, exactly the P4.M1.L1 principle applied at the disassembly stage.`}],applications:`Recognising genuine dependencies versus convention is exactly the reasoning skill the Repair Workshop's access-and-removal steps (before a component can be tested or replaced) rely on.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating every step in a guided sequence as an equally strict dependency.`,correction:`Some steps are genuine mechanical/electrical dependencies; others are convenient conventions — confusing the two can cause unnecessary difficulty on a real, differently-laid-out unit.`},{mistake:`Forcing the heat sink out while switches are still clamped to it.`,correction:`This risks damaging both the switches and the heat sink; unclamping must happen first, every time, because it is a true dependency.`},{mistake:`Disconnecting wires without documenting their original position or orientation.`,correction:`A correct removal SEQUENCE alone does not guarantee correct REASSEMBLY of every individual wire — documentation closes that gap.`}],demo:{title:`Follow the full disassembly sequence`,text:`Work through cover, fan, switches/heat sink, power board, magnetics and terminals in order.`,link:e.lab3d(`Disassembly sequence`,`lf3k`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Disassembly sequence`,`lf3k`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Unclamping switches before removing the heat sink is a...`,options:[`genuine mechanical dependency`,`arbitrary convention`,`step that can be skipped`,`step with no real reason`],answer:0,explain:`The switches are physically clamped to the heat sink.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Removing the fan before the heat sink is best described as...`,options:[`a sensible convention, not a strict dependency`,`mechanically impossible to do otherwise`,`the same kind of constraint as the switch-clamping dependency`,`required by electrical safety rules`],answer:0,explain:`It aids access but is not a hard mechanical requirement.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The PE stud is removed last among the terminals in disassembly because...`,options:[`it keeps the chassis earthed for as long as physically possible`,`it is the easiest terminal to access`,`it has no electrical function`,`terminal order has no real reasoning`],answer:0,explain:`The same earthing-safety principle from P4.M1.L1, applied here.`}],summary:[`The disassembly sequence follows cover, fan, switches/heat sink, power board, magnetics, then terminals.`,`Some steps are genuine mechanical or electrical DEPENDENCIES; others are sensible CONVENTIONS — the two are not the same.`,`The heat sink genuinely cannot be removed until the switches clamped to it are unclamped first.`,`The PE stud is removed last among the terminals, preserving the earth bond as long as possible.`],homework:{id:`P4.M2.L2.hw`,title:`Disassembly sequence reasoning practice`,brief:`Classify steps as dependency or convention and justify.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A real unit has its fan mounted somewhere that does NOT block the heat sink. Should the technician still remove it before the heat sink?`,options:[`Not necessarily — this ordering was a convention, not a hard dependency`,`Yes, always, with no exception`,`No, the fan should never be removed`,`The heat sink can never be removed in this case`],answer:0,explain:`Since this was convention rather than dependency, it can be adapted when the physical layout differs.`}],written:[{prompt:`A new technician follows the guided sequence step by step but struggles when a real unit's layout differs slightly from the model (for example, its fan is mounted elsewhere). Explain how understanding WHICH steps are dependencies versus conventions would have helped them adapt.`,model:`If the technician understands that the fan-before-heat-sink ordering in the guided sequence was a CONVENTION (chosen for convenience on that specific model's layout) rather than a DEPENDENCY (a mechanical necessity), they can recognise that a differently-positioned fan on a real unit does not block the same reasoning — the fan can be left until a more convenient point, since nothing else in the sequence actually depends on its removal order. By contrast, recognising that unclamping the switches before the heat sink IS a genuine dependency tells them this particular step cannot be skipped or reordered regardless of the unit's layout, because it reflects an unavoidable physical constraint (the switches are bolted to the sink) rather than a model-specific convenience. Learning to tell these apart, rather than following the sequence as one undifferentiated list of steps, is what allows safe adaptation to a real unit that does not exactly match the guided model.`}]}}},{id:`P4.M2.L3`,title:`Reassembly and functional test`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Reverse the disassembly sequence correctly, respecting the dependency relationships identified in P4.M2.L2.`,`Perform a staged functional test after reassembly rather than assuming correctness from appearance.`,`Explain why reassembly order is not simply "disassembly, reversed" in every single respect.`],introduction:`This closing lesson reverses the disassembly sequence into a correct reassembly, then performs a guided functional test — because a unit that LOOKS correctly reassembled is not the same as one CONFIRMED to work correctly.`,lecture:`### Reassembly: mostly reversed, with one deliberate exception

Reassembly follows the disassembly sequence in reverse for almost every step: terminals and PE stud first (PE FIRST this time, matching P4.M1.L1's reasoning exactly), then magnetics, power board, heat sink/switches, fan, and finally the cover. The one deliberate exception that is NOT simply "reversed" is the earth connection itself: it was removed LAST in disassembly and is reconnected FIRST in reassembly — the earth bond's priority is preserved at both ends of the procedure, not mechanically mirrored.

### Reassembly quality checks, step by step

Beyond simply reversing bolt-and-wire positions, reassembly includes real quality checks this lesson revisits from earlier modules: fresh insulating pads under every clamped switch (F4.M2.L10's compatibility/replacement reasoning — old pads are not reused), correct capacitor and diode polarity when the power board is refitted (a reversed electrolytic capacitor is a real, damaging mistake), and confirmed earth continuity (P4.M1.L1) once the earth stud is back in place, BEFORE any source is reconnected.

### Functional test: staged, not all-at-once

A functional test is not "plug everything in and switch it on." It is staged: confirm earth continuity first, then reconnect the battery alone and check for correct standby behaviour before enabling output, then bring up the output under no load, then finally under a real load — checking at each stage before proceeding to the next, exactly the incremental-verification discipline that prevents a single hidden reassembly mistake from causing a much larger failure.`,technical:`**Why insulating pads must be fresh, not reused.** F4.M2.L10 explained that a MOSFET/IGBT's insulating pad degrades with thermal cycling; reusing an old pad during reassembly can silently reintroduce exactly the thermal-isolation fault the original repair may have been trying to fix.

**Why capacitor polarity errors are a reassembly-specific risk.** Disassembly removes a charged, correctly-oriented part; reassembly is where a part can be refitted backwards if its orientation was not documented (P4.M2.L2's point about documenting wire/part positions) — this is precisely why documenting orientation during disassembly directly prevents a reassembly-stage fault.`,diagrams:[{id:`risk-hierarchy`,caption:`The reassembly sequence as the disassembly order reversed, with the earth-bond priority preserved rather than mechanically mirrored, followed by a staged functional test.`}],formulas:[{name:`Staged verification (concept)`,expr:`Confirm each stage (earth continuity → battery/standby → output no-load → output on load) before proceeding to the next`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Why PE is reconnected first, not last`,problem:`A technician reverses the ENTIRE disassembly sequence mechanically, including leaving PE for the very last reconnection step (mirroring where it was in disassembly's final step). Explain why this is wrong.`,steps:[`Disassembly removed PE LAST specifically to minimise the unearthed window`,`Reassembly should minimise the SAME unearthed window by reconnecting PE FIRST, not last`],answer:`Reconnecting PE last would leave every reassembled part effectively unearthed throughout nearly the entire reassembly process — exactly backwards from the safety intent; PE must be reconnected FIRST to restore the earth bond as early as possible, which is the one place reassembly is NOT a pure mechanical reversal of disassembly.`},{title:`Diagnosing a reassembly-introduced fault`,problem:`After reassembly, the staged functional test shows correct standby behaviour but the output stage fails only once a real load is applied, not under no-load. Suggest a reassembly-related explanation.`,steps:[`A no-load pass but load-dependent failure suggests the output stage mostly works but struggles under current`,`A reused insulating pad (F4.M2.L10) or a slightly loose power-switch clamp from reassembly could cause exactly this: adequate at low current/no load, but thermally or electrically marginal under real current`],answer:`This pattern is consistent with a reassembly-introduced thermal or clamping issue at the power switches (e.g. a reused, degraded insulating pad, or an under-torqued clamp) — exactly why the staged test catches a fault that a quick "powers on fine" check would have missed entirely.`}],applications:`This staged functional-test discipline is exactly what the Repair Workshop expects after any component replacement scenario, and it is the final, practical skill this module builds toward.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating reassembly as a purely mechanical reversal of disassembly in every respect.`,correction:`The earth connection is the deliberate exception: reconnected FIRST, not last, preserving the same safety priority at both ends of the procedure.`},{mistake:`Reusing an old insulating pad under a reassembled power switch.`,correction:`A degraded pad can silently reintroduce a thermal-isolation fault; fresh pads are required at reassembly (F4.M2.L10).`},{mistake:`Switching a freshly reassembled unit straight to full load without staged checks.`,correction:`A staged test (earth, then standby, then no-load output, then loaded output) catches a fault at the earliest, safest, least damaging stage.`}],demo:{title:`Reassemble and run the functional test`,text:`Reverse the sequence correctly and complete the staged functional test.`,link:e.lab3d(`Reassembly and functional test`,`lf3k`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Reassembly and functional test`,`lf3k`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`During reassembly, the earth (PE) connection is...`,options:[`reconnected first, not last`,`reconnected last, mirroring disassembly exactly`,`optional`,`reconnected at a random point`],answer:0,explain:`The one deliberate exception to mechanical reversal, preserving the earthing-safety priority.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A functional test should bring the unit up...`,options:[`in stages, checking at each stage before proceeding`,`straight to full load immediately`,`with no checks until the very end`,`only by visual inspection`],answer:0,explain:`Staged verification catches faults early and safely.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Reusing an old insulating pad on a reassembled power switch risks...`,options:[`silently reintroducing a thermal-isolation fault`,`improving thermal performance`,`having no effect at all`,`an immediate, obvious failure every time`],answer:0,explain:`Degradation from thermal cycling is not always visible, and its effect can be subtle until under real load.`}],summary:[`Reassembly reverses the disassembly sequence for almost every step, with the earth connection as the deliberate exception: reconnected first, not last.`,`Reassembly quality checks (fresh insulating pads, correct polarity, confirmed earth continuity) are real steps, not formalities.`,`A functional test is staged — earth continuity, then standby, then no-load output, then loaded output — each confirmed before the next.`,`A load-dependent fault after reassembly often points to a thermal or clamping issue introduced during reassembly itself.`],homework:{id:`P4.M2.L3.hw`,title:`Reassembly and functional test practice`,brief:`Apply staged-verification reasoning to a reassembly scenario.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The correct order for a staged functional test is...`,options:[`earth continuity, then standby, then no-load output, then loaded output`,`loaded output first, to save time`,`no-load output only, every time`,`standby only, with no further stages`],answer:0,explain:`Each stage confirms safety and correctness before the next, more demanding stage.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why the earth connection being reconnected FIRST during reassembly, while being removed LAST during disassembly, is not actually a contradiction but reflects the same underlying principle applied at both ends of the procedure.`,model:`The underlying principle is not "earth goes first" or "earth goes last" as fixed rules — it is "minimise the time the chassis is unearthed while other work is being done around it." During disassembly, everything else is being disconnected and removed, so keeping PE connected until last keeps the chassis earthed throughout the longest possible portion of that process. During reassembly, everything else is still being reconnected and refitted, so reconnecting PE first restores the earth bond as early as possible, again keeping the chassis earthed throughout the longest possible portion of that process. In both cases, the SAME safety goal — maximise earthed time during the procedure — produces an OPPOSITE position in the sequence (last versus first) simply because disassembly and reassembly are opposite directions of the same process. There is no contradiction; it is one consistent principle correctly applied in two different directions.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A failed bleeder resistor makes a "fixed wait time, no meter check" discharge rule unsafe because...`,options:[`real discharge time can become far longer than expected`,`capacitors never discharge at all in that case`,`the wait time becomes shorter`,`bleeder resistors cannot actually fail`],answer:0,explain:`The RC time constant depends on resistor value; a failed-open resistor extends it unpredictably.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Unclamping switches before removing the heat sink is a...`,options:[`genuine mechanical dependency`,`arbitrary convention`,`step that can always be skipped`,`electrical, not mechanical, requirement`],answer:0,explain:`The switches are physically clamped to the heat sink.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`During reassembly, the earth connection is reconnected...`,options:[`first, not last`,`last, mirroring disassembly exactly`,`at any convenient point`,`only if time permits`],answer:0,explain:`The deliberate exception preserving the earthing-safety priority.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A functional test should proceed...`,options:[`in stages, each confirmed before the next`,`straight to full load`,`by visual inspection only`,`with no particular order`],answer:0,explain:`Staged verification limits the consequence of any hidden fault.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Reusing an old insulating pad on a reassembled switch risks...`,options:[`silently reintroducing a thermal-isolation fault`,`improving performance`,`no measurable effect`,`an immediate failure on first power-up every time`],answer:0,explain:`Degradation from thermal cycling can be subtle and load-dependent.`}]},practicals:[{id:`P4.M2.P1`,title:`Isolation, discharge and disassembly`,description:`Isolate every source, confirm bus-capacitor discharge, and complete the guided disassembly sequence on the LF-3K model.`,link:e.lab3d(`Disassembly sequence`,`lf3k`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`lab3d:disassembly`}},{id:`P4.M2.P2`,title:`Reassembly and functional test`,description:`Reverse the sequence correctly and pass the staged functional test.`,link:e.lab3d(`Reassembly and functional test`,`lf3k`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`lab3d:reassembly`}}]},{id:`P4.M3`,title:`Identification`,summary:`Timed, scored component identification on a real board, and a direct comparison of a square-wave inverter against a hybrid inverter's architecture.`,lessons:[{id:`P4.M3.L1`,title:`Recognising components on a board`,minutes:20,content:{objectives:[`Identify at least 20 real inverter components by sight, under time pressure, in the 3D laboratory's identification challenge.`,`Use physical cues (size, markings, mounting, position) rather than labels alone to identify a part.`,`Explain why rapid, confident identification matters before any repair work begins.`],introduction:`This lesson is a direct, scored test of everything the architecture tour (P4.M1) built: can you identify a real inverter's components by SIGHT, quickly and correctly, without relying on labels being conveniently visible?`,lecture:`### Identification cues, beyond the label

A real component rarely announces itself with a convenient label reading "MOSFET" — it is identified by a combination of cues: physical size and package type (a TO-247 power device looks different from a small-signal transistor), markings (a datecode or part number silkscreened or printed on the body), mounting (clamped to a heat sink versus free-standing), and position in the circuit (near the gate drivers, or near the output terminals). F4's Component Library taught these cues one component at a time; this identification challenge tests recognising them together, under time pressure, exactly as a technician must when opening an unfamiliar unit.

### Why speed and confidence both matter

A technician who can eventually identify every part, given unlimited time, is not yet ready for real repair work — unlimited time is rarely available, and hesitation over basic identification slows every subsequent diagnostic step. Equally, confident but WRONG identification (mistaking an IGBT for a MOSFET, say) can lead to applying the wrong test procedure (F4.M2.L8) or ordering the wrong replacement part (F4.M2.L10) — so this challenge scores both speed and accuracy, not one alone.

### Common identification traps

Similar-looking parts in different roles are the most common source of error: two capacitors of visually similar size but very different voltage ratings (F4.M1.L2), a relay and a larger contactor that are electrically related but mechanically different (F4.M4.L3), or a MOSFET and an IGBT that can look superficially similar in some packages despite very different characteristics (F4.M2.L4-L5).`,technical:`**Datecodes and part numbers as the most reliable cue.** When present and legible, a component's printed part number or datecode is the single most reliable identification cue — far more reliable than package shape alone, which can be shared across genuinely different device types.

**Why position in the circuit is a secondary, not primary, cue.** Position (e.g. "near the heat sink") narrows the likely candidates (a power switch, not a signal-level IC) but does not, by itself, distinguish a MOSFET from an IGBT — both are commonly heat-sink-mounted. Position supports identification; it does not replace checking the part's actual marking.`,diagrams:[{id:`risk-hierarchy`,caption:`Identification cues in order of reliability: part marking/datecode (most reliable), package/size, mounting, and circuit position (most general, least specific).`}],formulas:[{name:`Identification scoring (concept)`,expr:`Score reflects both the number correctly identified AND time taken, not accuracy alone`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Resolving an identification ambiguity`,problem:`Two similarly-sized devices sit on a heat sink. One is marked clearly; the other's marking is worn illegible. How should each be identified?`,steps:[`The clearly marked device is identified directly from its printed part number — the most reliable cue`,`The illegible device cannot be identified with full confidence from marking alone; package shape, pin configuration and circuit position become the best available secondary cues, with lower certainty`],answer:`The marked device is identified with high confidence from its part number; the unmarked device is identified with LOWER confidence from secondary cues, and ideally flagged for confirmation (e.g. by context, circuit tracing, or seeking a clearer marking elsewhere on the part) before being treated as certain.`},{title:`Why wrong-but-confident identification is worse than slow-but-correct`,problem:`Explain, using F4.M2.L10, why confidently misidentifying an IGBT as a MOSFET is more costly than simply taking longer to identify it correctly.`,steps:[`F4.M2.L10 explained that sourcing a replacement depends on correctly knowing the device TYPE and its exact ratings`,`A confident misidentification leads directly to ordering or fitting the WRONG replacement part, a compatibility failure, not just a delay`],answer:`Taking longer to identify correctly costs time; confidently misidentifying costs a wrong replacement part fitted, a real compatibility failure that can damage the circuit or the new part itself — which is why this challenge's scoring values accuracy as much as speed, not speed alone.`}],applications:`This identification skill is the direct prerequisite for every F4 test procedure and every Repair Workshop scenario — you cannot correctly test or replace a part you have misidentified.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Relying on package shape alone to distinguish a MOSFET from an IGBT.`,correction:`Package shape can be shared across different device types; the part's printed marking is the more reliable cue when legible.`},{mistake:`Treating circuit position as sufficient to confirm a specific device type.`,correction:`Position narrows the likely candidates but does not replace checking the actual marking — many different device types share similar positions (e.g. heat-sink-mounted).`},{mistake:`Prioritising speed over accuracy in identification.`,correction:`A confident but wrong identification leads to the wrong test procedure or the wrong replacement part — a real, costly compatibility failure, not just a minor error.`}],demo:{title:`Take the identification challenge`,text:`Identify at least 20 real components by sight, under time pressure.`,link:e.lab3d(`Identification challenge`,`lf3k`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Identification challenge`,`lf3k`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The most reliable single identification cue, when legible, is...`,options:[`the part's printed marking or datecode`,`its physical package shape alone`,`its position in the circuit alone`,`its colour`],answer:0,explain:`Package and position can be shared across different device types; the marking is specific.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A confident but WRONG identification is costly mainly because...`,options:[`it leads to the wrong test procedure or the wrong replacement part being used`,`it takes longer than being unsure`,`it has no real consequence`,`it only affects the scoring, not real repair work`],answer:0,explain:`Wrong identification leads directly to a real compatibility failure.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Circuit position (e.g. "mounted on the heat sink") is best used as...`,options:[`a cue that narrows likely candidates, not a confirmation of exact device type`,`the single most reliable identification method`,`irrelevant to identification`,`proof the part is a MOSFET specifically`],answer:0,explain:`Many different device types can share a similar position.`}],summary:[`Component identification relies on multiple cues together: marking, package, mounting, and circuit position.`,`A printed part number or datecode, when legible, is the most reliable single cue.`,`Circuit position narrows likely candidates but does not, by itself, confirm an exact device type.`,`Confident misidentification is more costly than slow correct identification, since it leads to real compatibility failures in testing or replacement.`],homework:{id:`P4.M3.L1.hw`,title:`Identification reasoning practice`,brief:`Apply the cue-reliability hierarchy to a short scenario.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`When a part's marking is legible, the correct priority is to...`,options:[`trust the marking over package shape or position alone`,`ignore the marking and rely on position`,`trust package shape over the marking`,`guess based on colour`],answer:0,explain:`Marking is the most specific and reliable cue when available.`}],written:[{prompt:`A trainee argues that since they can always look up a part's full identity later from its datasheet once it is removed, there is no real value in being able to identify it quickly BY SIGHT while it is still in the circuit. Explain the flaw in this reasoning.`,model:`Identifying a part correctly while it is STILL in the circuit matters for reasons that removing it first does not solve: knowing what a part is, and roughly how it should behave, is what tells a technician which test procedure to run on it in the first place (F4's test-procedure lessons are organised by device type), which safety precautions apply before touching it (a capacitor bank is not the same safety case as a resistor), and whether it is even the right suspect for the fault being diagnosed before time is spent removing it at all. Waiting until after removal to identify a part means potentially removing, testing, or disturbing the wrong component before the diagnosis is even clear — by-sight identification while still in place is what makes efficient, safe, targeted diagnosis possible, not merely a shortcut for avoiding a datasheet lookup.`}]}}},{id:`P4.M3.L2`,title:`Comparing a square-wave and a hybrid inverter`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Compare the SQ-1K (square-wave) and LF-3K (hybrid, modified/pure-sine-capable) model architectures directly.`,`Explain which component families appear in one model but not the other, and why.`,`Relate each architectural difference to the output-waveform-quality and feature differences between the two designs.`],introduction:`This closing lesson compares the two model inverters side by side: the simpler SQ-1K (a square-wave design) against the more complex LF-3K (a hybrid design with MPPT charging and sensing) — not as an abstract comparison, but using the real architecture each model exposes.`,lecture:`### What the simpler design omits

The SQ-1K model has no MPPT charger stage at all — no IGBTs, no dedicated PWM oscillator for charging, no PV input terminals. Its power board carries a far simpler single-purpose oscillator IC (SG3525-family, exactly the same family used in LF-3K's charger stage, interestingly, but here driving the MAIN output instead) plus a bleeder resistor and a rectifier diode — a design built to do ONE job (square-wave output from a battery) and nothing else.

### What the more complex design adds, and why

The LF-3K adds, on top of the same core H-bridge/transformer/output-filter architecture: a PV input path with its own fuse, breaker and dedicated IGBT-based charger stage (for MPPT charging from solar, F4.M3.L2's LF-vs-HF discussion and F4.M5's sensing/control modules); a Hall-effect current sensor and voltage-sense module (closed-loop feedback, F4.M5.L1-L2, which the simpler SQ-1K design does not need since it has no charging function to regulate); and a microcontroller alongside its PWM controller, where SQ-1K uses only a single PWM oscillator IC with no programmable control at all.

### Connecting architecture to output quality

These additions are not incidental — F4.M5.L1's control-philosophy comparison explained that a microcontroller's flexibility is what makes SPWM-based, higher-quality sine-shaped output possible, something a fixed-function oscillator alone cannot produce. The SQ-1K's simpler square-wave output and the LF-3K's more sophisticated output are a direct, visible consequence of exactly this architectural difference, not an unrelated design choice.`,technical:`**Why SQ-1K still uses an SG3525-family IC.** The SAME dedicated-PWM-IC family (F4.M5.L1) appears in both models, but for different jobs: driving the MAIN output directly in SQ-1K's simpler design, versus handling only the SECONDARY charger stage in LF-3K's more complex design, where a microcontroller handles the main output instead. This shows the same component family can suit very different roles depending on what a design as a whole needs.

**Cost and complexity trade-off, made visible.** Every additional part identified in LF-3K that SQ-1K lacks (PV terminals, breaker, IGBTs, current sensor, voltage-sense module, microcontroller) represents real added cost and complexity — a visible, physical illustration of why a square-wave-only design remains a legitimate, simpler, lower-cost choice for applications that do not need solar charging or sine-quality output.`,diagrams:[{id:`design-chain`,caption:`The SQ-1K and LF-3K architectures side by side, showing which component families the hybrid design adds and how each addition supports a specific extra capability.`}],formulas:[{name:`Capability-to-architecture mapping (concept)`,expr:`Each extra capability (MPPT charging, closed-loop feedback, programmable SPWM output) corresponds to a specific, identifiable set of extra components`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Predicting a missing capability from missing parts`,problem:`A technician opens an unfamiliar inverter and finds no current sensor, no voltage-sense module, and only a single fixed-function PWM IC with no microcontroller. Based on this comparison, what can be inferred about this unit's likely capabilities?`,steps:[`No current/voltage sensing suggests no closed-loop feedback control, similar to SQ-1K`,`A single fixed-function PWM IC with no microcontroller suggests no SPWM-based sine-quality output and no MPPT charging capability`],answer:`This unit is architecturally closer to the simpler SQ-1K design: likely a square-wave (or similarly simple) output with no solar-charging capability — an inference drawn directly from which component families are ABSENT, exactly the comparison this lesson builds.`},{title:`Explaining a shared component family's different role`,problem:`Both models use an SG3525-family PWM IC, but F4.M5.L1 might seem to suggest a microcontroller is always the "better" choice. Resolve this apparent tension.`,steps:[`F4.M5.L1 explained each control technology is matched to the job it suits, not that one is universally better`,`In SQ-1K, the PWM IC drives the only output the design needs; in LF-3K, it is relegated to a secondary charger role because the main output now needs the microcontroller's flexibility`],answer:`There is no real tension: the SAME component can be the right choice for a simple, single-purpose job (SQ-1K's main output) while being only appropriate for a secondary role once a more demanding job (LF-3K's SPWM main output) requires different flexibility — matching technology to job, not ranking one technology as universally superior, is the consistent principle.`}],applications:`This architecture-to-capability comparison is exactly the reasoning the Repair Workshop and the Component Library's comparison guides (mosfet-vs-igbt, pwm-vs-mcu) rely on when explaining why a specific design uses the parts it does.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a more complex design is simply a "better" version of a simpler one in every respect.`,correction:`Added complexity (MPPT charging, closed-loop feedback, SPWM output) brings real capability, but also real added cost — the simpler design remains the right choice when those capabilities are not needed.`},{mistake:`Assuming the same IC family always performs the same role across different designs.`,correction:`The SG3525-family IC drives the main output in SQ-1K but only a secondary charger stage in LF-3K — its role depends on what the rest of the design needs from it.`},{mistake:`Inferring a unit's full capability from a single component alone.`,correction:`Capability is inferred from the PATTERN of components present (or absent) together, not any single part in isolation.`}],demo:{title:`Compare SQ-1K and LF-3K`,text:`Tour both models side by side and identify the architectural differences.`,link:e.lab3d(`Model comparison`,`sq1k`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`Square-wave model`,`sq1k`),e.lab3d(`Hybrid model`,`lf3k`),e.cmp(`PWM controller vs microcontroller`,`pwm-vs-mcu`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The SQ-1K model lacks an MPPT charger stage mainly because...`,options:[`it is a simpler design built for one job (square-wave output from a battery) only`,`IGBTs cannot physically fit on its board`,`MPPT charging is impossible with any square-wave design`,`it has no power board at all`],answer:0,explain:`A deliberate scope/cost difference, not a technical impossibility.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The same SG3525-family PWM IC appears in both models because...`,options:[`the same component family can suit different roles depending on what the rest of the design needs`,`LF-3K copied SQ-1K's design exactly`,`it is the only PWM IC that exists`,`PWM ICs are never role-specific`],answer:0,explain:`Role, not just component identity, depends on the overall design.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Inferring a unit's capability from its components should rely on...`,options:[`the pattern of components present or absent together, not any single part alone`,`a single component in isolation`,`colour and size alone`,`the manufacturer's name only`],answer:0,explain:`Capability is a property of the whole architecture, not one isolated part.`}],summary:[`SQ-1K is a simpler, single-purpose square-wave design; LF-3K adds MPPT charging, closed-loop sensing and SPWM-capable control.`,`Each added capability in LF-3K corresponds to a specific, identifiable set of extra components absent from SQ-1K.`,`The same component family (e.g. an SG3525-family PWM IC) can serve different roles depending on what the overall design needs from it.`,`Architectural complexity is a real capability-versus-cost trade-off, not simply "better" versus "worse".`],homework:{id:`P4.M3.L2.hw`,title:`Architecture comparison practice`,brief:`Apply capability-to-architecture-pattern reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A unit with no current sensor, no voltage-sense module and a single fixed-function PWM IC is architecturally closest to...`,options:[`the simpler SQ-1K design`,`the more complex LF-3K design`,`neither; this combination is impossible`,`a design with MPPT charging`],answer:0,explain:`The absence of sensing and programmable control points to the simpler architecture.`}],written:[{prompt:`A customer asks why they should pay more for a hybrid (LF-3K-style) inverter instead of a cheaper square-wave (SQ-1K-style) unit, if "they both just turn DC into AC". Using this lesson’s comparison, explain what the extra cost is actually paying for.`,model:`Both designs do convert DC to AC, but the extra cost of a hybrid-style unit pays for specific, identifiable additional capabilities that a square-wave design cannot provide: an MPPT charging stage (with its own IGBTs, PV terminals, fuse and breaker) that lets the unit charge efficiently from a solar array rather than requiring a separate charge controller; closed-loop current and voltage sensing that allows the unit to regulate and protect itself actively rather than running open-loop; and a microcontroller-based control system capable of generating SPWM, which produces a much cleaner, sine-shaped output waveform suited to sensitive electronics, compared with a square-wave design's simpler, harsher output. The price difference is not arbitrary — it reflects a real, traceable set of extra components and capabilities, each serving a specific function the simpler design was never built to provide.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The most reliable single component identification cue, when legible, is...`,options:[`the printed marking or datecode`,`package shape alone`,`circuit position alone`,`colour`],answer:0,explain:`Marking is specific; package and position can be shared across device types.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A confidently WRONG identification is costly mainly because...`,options:[`it leads to the wrong test procedure or replacement part`,`it is slower than correct identification`,`it has no real consequence`,`it only affects a score, never real repair work`],answer:0,explain:`Wrong identification causes real downstream compatibility failures.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`SQ-1K lacks an MPPT charger stage mainly because...`,options:[`it is a simpler, single-purpose design`,`MPPT is technically impossible on any square-wave unit`,`it has no power board`,`IGBTs cannot be manufactured`],answer:0,explain:`A deliberate scope and cost decision.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The same PWM IC family appearing in both models shows that...`,options:[`the same component can suit different roles depending on the overall design`,`LF-3K is a copy of SQ-1K`,`PWM ICs are never role-specific`,`this is a design error`],answer:0,explain:`Role depends on what the rest of the design needs, not the component alone.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Inferring a unit's capability should rely on...`,options:[`the pattern of components present or absent together`,`one isolated component`,`colour and size`,`the manufacturer's name`],answer:0,explain:`Capability is a property of the whole architecture.`}]},practicals:[{id:`P4.M3.P1`,title:`Component identification challenge`,description:`Identify at least 20 real inverter components by sight, under time pressure.`,link:e.lab3d(`Identification challenge`,`lf3k`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`lab3d:identify`}}]}],exam:{pass:.8,minutes:40,attempts:3,cooldownHours:24,questions:[{id:`e1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The protective earth connection is removed...`,options:[`last, and reconnected first`,`first, and reconnected last`,`at any convenient point`,`only on models without a battery`],answer:0,explain:`Minimising the unearthed window.`},{id:`e2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A real power board mixing MOSFETs and IGBTs most likely reflects...`,options:[`each device family used where its strengths suit a different stage`,`a manufacturing error`,`random selection`,`cost-cutting with no technical basis`],answer:0,explain:`Deliberate device selection per F4.M2.`},{id:`e3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The LF-3K transformer's substantial mass for its power rating directly illustrates...`,options:[`why line-frequency transformers are larger than HF-link designs of equal power`,`a design flaw`,`an oversized part`,`nothing relevant to magnetics design`],answer:0,explain:`A direct physical consequence of line-frequency operation.`},{id:`e4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A board using both a microcontroller and a dedicated PWM IC does so because...`,options:[`each control philosophy is matched to the job it suits best`,`one is a redundant leftover`,`microcontrollers cannot generate PWM`,`PWM ICs cannot coexist with microcontrollers`],answer:0,explain:`Deliberate matching of control technology to function.`},{id:`e5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse protects a circuit...`,options:[`independently of what the sensing/control system reports`,`only with sensor confirmation`,`only when commanded by firmware`,`never without sensing input`],answer:0,explain:`Protection devices act on actual current, independent of sensing.`},{id:`e6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A "wait a fixed time" discharge rule with no meter check is unsafe because...`,options:[`a failed bleeder resistor can make real discharge time much longer than expected`,`capacitors never discharge`,`5 minutes is always excessive`,`bleeder resistors cannot fail`],answer:0,explain:`Discharge time depends on R×C; a failed resistor changes R unpredictably.`},{id:`e7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Confirming one bus-capacitor bank is discharged...`,options:[`says nothing definite about a separate bank's state`,`confirms every bank in the unit is safe`,`confirms the AC output is isolated`,`means the bleeder resistors can be removed`],answer:0,explain:`Each voltage domain must be confirmed independently.`},{id:`e8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Unclamping switches before removing the heat sink is a...`,options:[`genuine mechanical dependency`,`arbitrary convention`,`step that can always be skipped`,`purely electrical requirement`],answer:0,explain:`The switches are physically clamped to the heat sink.`},{id:`e9`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Removing the fan before the heat sink is best described as...`,options:[`a sensible convention, not a strict dependency`,`mechanically impossible to do otherwise`,`the same kind of constraint as switch-clamping`,`required by electrical safety rules`],answer:0,explain:`Aids access, but is not a hard mechanical requirement.`},{id:`e10`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`During reassembly, the earth connection is reconnected...`,options:[`first, not last`,`last, mirroring disassembly exactly`,`at any convenient point`,`only if time permits`],answer:0,explain:`The deliberate exception preserving the earthing-safety priority.`},{id:`e11`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A functional test after reassembly should proceed...`,options:[`in stages, each confirmed before the next`,`straight to full load`,`by visual inspection only`,`with no particular order`],answer:0,explain:`Staged verification limits the consequence of any hidden fault.`},{id:`e12`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The most reliable single component identification cue, when legible, is...`,options:[`the printed marking or datecode`,`package shape alone`,`circuit position alone`,`colour`],answer:0,explain:`Marking is specific; package and position can be shared across device types.`},{id:`e13`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A confidently WRONG component identification is costly mainly because...`,options:[`it leads to the wrong test procedure or replacement part`,`it is slower than correct identification`,`it has no real consequence`,`it only affects a score`],answer:0,explain:`Wrong identification causes real downstream compatibility failures.`},{id:`e14`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`SQ-1K lacks an MPPT charger stage mainly because...`,options:[`it is a simpler, single-purpose design`,`MPPT is technically impossible on any square-wave unit`,`it has no power board`,`IGBTs cannot be manufactured`],answer:0,explain:`A deliberate scope and cost decision.`},{id:`e15`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Inferring a unit's capability from its components should rely on...`,options:[`the pattern of components present or absent together`,`one isolated component`,`colour and size`,`the manufacturer's name`],answer:0,explain:`Capability is a property of the whole architecture, not one isolated part.`}]}};export{t as P4};